Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bhaskix vendors third-party source into third_party/ only, and lists every such
component here. Each carries its own license text and a PROVENANCE.md naming the
upstream, the version taken, the copyright holder, the license it was taken
under, and what was changed. This paragraph said "does not vendor third-party
source code" until 2026-08-22; it stopped being true with RFC 0038, and a NOTICE
that overstates a project's independence is worse than one that names what it
took.

  xHCI layouts  (third_party/xhci/)
    Adapted from https://github.com/rust-osdev/xhci, version 0.9.2.
    Copyright (c) 2021 Hiroki Tokunaga.
    Upstream is licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; taken under Apache-2.0, which is
    the license Bhaskix already uses, so no second license enters this tree.
    Adapted rather than copied: all five upstream dependencies were removed and
    their work written out. See third_party/xhci/PROVENANCE.md.
    Linked into the Bhaskix kernel.

  SLH-DSA test vectors  (third_party/acvp-slh-dsa/)
    From NIST ACVP-Server, https://github.com/usnistgov/ACVP-Server: the
    SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s signature-verification, key-generation and signing
    vectors, as of upstream commit
    112690e8484d. Provided by NIST under the notice kept intact in
    third_party/acvp-slh-dsa/LICENSE-NIST.md; selected, trimmed and
    re-encoded on 2026-10-29, as PROVENANCE.md there records.
    Test data for bhaskix-bootsig's host tests; linked into nothing.

The following components are fetched by the scripts named, each pinned there,
and distributed under their own licenses. No part of any of them is linked into
the Bhaskix kernel. Until 2026-10-29 this list named Limine alone, while the
release ISO also carried Go's runtime; tools/check-notice.py now refuses a
fetching script that does not name its component here.

  Limine bootloader  (tools/setup-dev.sh, branch v8.x-binary)
    https://github.com/limine-bootloader/limine
    Copyright (C) 2019-2025 mintsuki and contributors.
    Licensed under the BSD 2-Clause License.
    Used only as a boot protocol implementation. The Bhaskix kernel consumes
    bhaskix_boot::Handoff, a project-owned structure; see docs/architecture.md §1.
    ~~Scheduled for replacement by bhaskixboot.efi in Phase 2~~ -- replaced on
    UEFI by bhaskixboot.efi, the project's own loader, on 2026-08-18; Limine
    remains the BIOS path and boots the ISO. Its binaries are in every ISO,
    so each release ships its license as LICENSE-limine.

  Go  (tools/fetch-go.sh, the release named there)
    https://go.dev
    Copyright 2009 The Go Authors.
    Licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License.
    The toolchain that builds bin/httpd, an unmodified Go net/http program run
    through the Linux personality; Go's runtime and standard library are
    compiled into that program, which the release ISO carries. Each release
    ships Go's license as LICENSE-go.

  BusyBox  (tools/fetch-busybox.sh, Ubuntu's busybox-static package)
    https://busybox.net
    Copyright many authors, 1998-2015.
    Licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 2.
    Used by the test images only, unmodified. The release leaves it out --
    tools/release-dist.sh refuses an ISO that carries it -- because shipping it
    would oblige publishing its source.

  sbsigntool  (tools/ceremony-kit.sh, Ubuntu's sbsigntool package)
    git://kernel.ubuntu.com/jk/sbsigntool (the source its Debian copyright file names)
    Copyright 2012 Canonical Ltd, and others.
    Licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later, with an
    OpenSSL exception; parts under the GPL 3+, the LGPL 2+ and CC0.
    Carried, unmodified, on the removable medium of the release-key ceremony
    (docs/release-keys.md) to the project lead's offline machine. It is not
    part of any release.

Test lanes also install, from Ubuntu's archive into local containers that are
never distributed, swtpm, virt-firmware, pesign, sbsigntool and OpenSSL; each
is pinned in tools/swtpm.sh or tests/qemu/release-keys-test.sh.

